# The cities corpus: somewhere that was a place, before 500 CE.
#
# Coordinates come back with every row rather than being filtered here.
# Wikidata's geo functions are slow and a bounding box is an editorial
# decision, not a data one — drawing it locally means changing the region
# without asking the service again. `pipeline/region.mjs` does that.
#
# `country` is deliberately not used. For an archaeological site Wikidata
# usually gives the modern country, but for the interesting ones it gives the
# ancient rulers instead — Babylon's P17 is twelve dated statements and not
# one of them is Iraq — so filtering on it drops exactly the cities worth
# having.
#
# The five OPTIONAL columns are evidence for a cut, not part of one. Ranked by
# sitelinks the answer to this query opens London, Jerusalem, Edirne, Venice —
# ancient foundations whose articles are about the living city, which the
# founded-after-500 rule cannot see because they were founded before it. What
# separates them from Ur is that somebody still governs them, so the query
# returns whether anybody does and `pipeline/seed.mjs` decides what that means.
# The last two are the monument cut, which a title regex cannot make on its
# own: it knows a `Temple of …` is not a city and has never heard of the
# Colosseum. One counts settlement classes and one counts monument classes, and
# they are counted separately rather than subtracted because a place with
# neither is not a monument — it is unclassified, which is a different answer
# and belongs to a person. Megiddo, Qumran, Skara Brae, Jarmo and Mehrgarh all
# land there, and all five are settlements.
# Row selection is unchanged: every one is OPTIONAL, and the 49 in
# `site/cities.yaml` came back from this query before these were added.
#
# A FOUNDING DATE IS NOT EVIDENCE OF A CITY, AND DEMANDING ONE COST US HALF
#
# `wdt:P571` was a required triple, and that quietly made this query blindest
# to exactly what it is for. 25 of the 49 in `site/cities.yaml` never came back
# from it — Nineveh, Memphis, Palmyra, Nippur, Eridu, Nimrud, Hattusa, Sidon —
# and nine in ten of those are missing for the same reason: Wikidata has no
# inception date for them. Nobody knows when Nineveh was founded. That is a
# fact about the oldest cities on earth, not a reason to exclude them.
#
# So the date is OPTIONAL, but not for everything, because `human settlement`
# with no date is every village on the planet — relaxing it for all fourteen
# classes returns 77,327 rows. The split is by class:
#
#   INHERENTLY ANCIENT — ancient city, archaeological site, city-state, polis,
#   oppidum, tell. Nothing is called these unless it is already over. An
#   undated one is an undated ancient place, so it comes back and the cuts in
#   `pipeline/cuts.mjs` judge it on the other evidence.
#
#   GENERIC — city, human settlement, town, village, big city, port city,
#   capital city. These describe the living as readily as the dead, so a date
#   before 500 is still required to distinguish Ur from Ulaanbaatar.
#
# `lost city` is the one that had to move across. It reads as ancient and is
# not: Wikidata files Chernobyl under it, undated, and Chernobyl duly arrived
# at 106 sitelinks ranking above Mohenjo-daro. Abandonment is not antiquity.
# It stays in the settlement evidence below, where saying "people lived here"
# is all it is being asked to say; it is only dropped from the set that
# excuses a missing date. A genuinely ancient lost city carries `ancient city`
# or `archaeological site` too, and comes back on that.
#
# What no rule fixes: Samarra and Angkor are undated in Wikidata and founded
# after 500, so nothing here can see that they are too late. They arrive as
# candidates and belong in the hand-written `rejected` block, which is what it
# is for. That is the trade the relaxation makes — Nineveh is undated for the
# same reason Samarra is, and no query can want the first without the second.
#
# The second half of the class cut mattered too, independently of the date.
# `?kind` admitted three classes while the settlement test below reads
# thirteen, so a place classed only `city-state` or `polis` could never match
# however well it scored on everything else. Troy is a `polis` with 121
# sitelinks and a date of −2999 — it would rank fourth in the whole queue —
# and this query could not see it. Neither could it see Ephesus, Knossos or
# Lagash, which is one of the 49.
#
# 395 rows became 2,771, and all 49 of the cities already in the corpus are now
# among them; 25 were not before. The cuts do the rest, and what they cannot
# judge they say so about rather than dropping.
#
#   curl -G https://query.wikidata.org/sparql \
#     --data-urlencode query@pipeline/select-cities.rq \
#     -H 'Accept: text/csv' -H 'User-Agent: … (you@example.com)' > cities.csv

SELECT DISTINCT ?title ?sitelinks (MIN(?inception) AS ?from) (SAMPLE(?coord) AS ?where)
       (COUNT(DISTINCT ?twin) AS ?twins)      # P190, twinned administrative body
       (COUNT(DISTINCT ?mayor) AS ?mayors)    # P6, head of government
       (MAX(?pop) AS ?people)                 # P1082 — historical for Babylon, current for London
       (COUNT(DISTINCT ?settlement) AS ?settled)      # P31 says somebody lived here
       (COUNT(DISTINCT ?built) AS ?monumental)        # P31 says somebody built one thing here
WHERE {
  # All fourteen settlement classes, which is the same list the settlement
  # evidence below reads — a place is a row here if Wikidata calls it any of
  # them. What splits them is not this block but the FILTER at the foot of it:
  # six of the fourteen excuse a missing founding date and the rest do not.
  # `lost city` is in here and not in that six; the head of this file says why.
  VALUES ?kind {
    wd:Q15661340 wd:Q839954 wd:Q133442 wd:Q148837 wd:Q192601 wd:Q2974842 wd:Q674413
    wd:Q515 wd:Q486972 wd:Q3957 wd:Q532 wd:Q1549591 wd:Q2264924 wd:Q5119
  }

  ?item wdt:P31 ?kind ;
        wikibase:sitelinks ?sitelinks ;
        wdt:P625 ?coord .

  OPTIONAL { ?item wdt:P571 ?inception }

  ?article schema:about ?item ;
           schema:isPartOf <https://en.wikipedia.org/> ;
           schema:name ?title .

  OPTIONAL { ?item wdt:P190 ?twin }
  OPTIONAL { ?item wdt:P6 ?mayor }
  OPTIONAL { ?item wdt:P1082 ?pop }

  # Every real city in the answer carries one of these: ancient city, city,
  # city-state, polis, human settlement, town, village, oppidum, lost city, big
  # city, port city, capital city, tell. Enumerated rather than walked, because
  # the exact class is the claim being read and a subclass of it is not.
  OPTIONAL {
    ?item wdt:P31 ?settlement .
    VALUES ?settlement {
      wd:Q15661340 wd:Q515 wd:Q133442 wd:Q148837 wd:Q486972 wd:Q3957 wd:Q532
      wd:Q192601 wd:Q2974842 wd:Q1549591 wd:Q2264924 wd:Q5119 wd:Q674413
    }
  }

  # And the other way: monument, temple, pyramid, tomb, amphitheatre, thermae,
  # aqueduct, geoglyph, column, triumphal arch, museum, church, tumulus,
  # necropolis. Walked with P279*, because here the subclasses are the point —
  # the Colosseum is a `Roman amphitheatre` and Stonehenge a `henge`, and
  # enumerating every leaf is how the list goes stale. The walk was free at 395
  # rows; at 2,771 it is most of a query that now takes 45–60 seconds, which is
  # close enough to the service's own limit that a run does occasionally come
  # back `upstream request timeout`. That is a retry, not a fault — and it is
  # the reason `seed.mjs --from cities.csv` exists, so a good answer can be
  # saved and re-seeded from without asking again.
  OPTIONAL {
    ?item wdt:P31/wdt:P279* ?built .
    VALUES ?built {
      wd:Q4989906 wd:Q44539 wd:Q12516 wd:Q381885 wd:Q54831 wd:Q6581615 wd:Q474
      wd:Q45791 wd:Q4817 wd:Q143912 wd:Q33506 wd:Q16970 wd:Q34023 wd:Q200141
    }
  }

  FILTER(?sitelinks > 8)

  # An inherently ancient class may arrive without a date; a generic one may
  # not. `?kind` is bound per class statement, so an item carrying several is
  # tested on each and survives if any one of them lets it through — which is
  # what "Wikidata calls this a polis somewhere" ought to mean.
  #
  # `isLiteral` is not decoration. Wikidata records "we know it has one and do
  # not know what it is" as a statement with a blank node for a value, so P571
  # comes back BOUND and is not a date — `YEAR()` of it raises, the filter goes
  # false, and the row vanishes with no indication why. Baalbek is dated that
  # way and Baalbek is one of the 49. An unknown date is no date.
  FILTER(
    ( ?kind IN (wd:Q15661340, wd:Q839954, wd:Q133442, wd:Q148837, wd:Q192601,
                wd:Q674413)
      && (!BOUND(?inception) || !isLiteral(?inception) || YEAR(?inception) < 500) )
    || ( BOUND(?inception) && isLiteral(?inception) && YEAR(?inception) < 500 )
  )
}
GROUP BY ?title ?sitelinks
ORDER BY DESC(?sitelinks)
